<Layout omitEditorialMetadata title="Build a Practical Social Media Strategy | Holy Webs" description="A useful strategy connects your audience, content and available time to a business goal." canonicalUrl="https://holywebs.com/resources/blog/how-to-develop-a-winning-social-media-strategy/"><div class="migration-page"><header><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / <a href="/resources/blog/">Blog</a></nav><h1>Build a Practical Social Media Strategy</h1></header><article><section><h2>Choose an outcome</h2><p>A useful strategy connects your audience, content and available time to a business goal. Decide whether you are explaining services, supporting referrals, answering customers or generating inquiries. Choose a measure that fits that goal rather than defaulting to follower growth.</p></section><section><h2>Learn from your customers</h2><p>Review real questions, objections and buying decisions. Choose channels where relevant customers already interact with the business. Do not infer a person’s interests from age or gender alone. Competitor accounts can suggest unanswered questions, but should not determine your voice.</p></section><section><h2>Make the profile useful</h2><p>Keep the business name, service area, contact path and description accurate. A visitor should understand what you offer and where to go next. Use consistent branding while checking that images remain readable in each placement.</p></section><section><h2>Plan a manageable calendar</h2><p>Build recurring themes around project examples, customer questions, team introductions and seasonal service needs. Record the topic, asset, author, approver and publishing date. Obtain permission for customer photos. Leave room for real business updates instead of filling every slot with generic posts.</p></section><section><h2>Budget time as well as money</h2><p>Organic publishing still takes labor. Decide who captures photos, writes, approves and responds. Keep paid campaign budgets separate so you can assess their contribution. Start with a schedule the team can sustain.</p></section><section><h2>Review and adjust</h2><p>Compare similar periods and explain changes in spend, seasonality or posting activity. Review qualified inquiries and customer feedback alongside exposure. Choose one improvement for the next cycle, name its owner and set a review date. No strategy guarantees results.</p></section><section><h2>Plan your next step</h2><p>Start with your <a href="/resources/blog/defining-your-websites-purpose-and-target-audience/">website purpose and audience</a>, or <a href="/contact/">contact Holy Webs</a> to discuss your priorities.</p></section></article></div>
